    "notes": "Reviewed approximate full-site fit on the unchanged161-square/6m standard DEM. Thirteen original mapped components retain97 vertices, including eight separate column centres. Six front columns face northeast, with two short rear returns; the ordinary front inscription photograph resolves the signed orientation. Geometry retains raw east/south coordinates at heading0, with a35.5237-degree internal architectural rotation. The occupied podium top clears every rendered grid cell intersecting its bounds by at least0.200001m: all81 bounding grid vertices were checked, so triangle interpolation cannot rise through it. Deep retaining sides and bounded chamber recesses enclose the native ground without any terrain/water opening. Full-site loading replaces13 authored records and preserves1932 independent tile buildings exactly. All2831 independent buildings in the retained extract are outside the replacement envelope;328 nearby records are retained in source, including the13 controls. Native water remains unchanged at127038.71519859218 square metres. Failed loads preserve1945 native tile buildings; eviction releases model geometry. Synthetic10m partial residency clips away the remote columns, entablature and much of the podium while preserving original ground under and outside the clipped model. Full architecture requires surrounding tiles; the partial fixture does not establish architectural completeness. The surrounding unmodeled Forum ruins still use the generic procedural renderer and are not historical reconstructions. Height, chamber placement, damage and stone profiles remain approximate; no surveyed thresholds are claimed."
  },
  "fidelityReview": {
    "target": "medium-fi",
    "status": "passed",
    "notes": "Accepted for the specific medium-fi present-day Temple of Saturn ruin and approximate standard-map/DEM placement. Thirteen mapped components retain 97 original vertices, including eight separate column centres. Ordinary northeast front and western reverse photos establish six front columns, two short returns, smooth granite shafts, Ionic capitals, the front restoration inscription and reused ornate rear band. Geometry uses raw east/south coordinates at heading 0, with a 35.5237-degree internal rotation. The surviving podium envelope is approximately 27×39m and the front column span about 19m. Podium mass, shaft/capital heights, 17.5m entablature top and irregular 19.15m upper core above the lower base datum are picture-guided estimates. Broken profiles, selected cornice blocks, simplified Ionic volutes, block lettering and three recessed chamber niches do not reproduce exact stone joints or a measured chamber plan. Granite lettering backs approximate visible carved contrast with a shared graph. No roof, full pediment, missing cella, buried rooms, restored original temple or independent Forum monument is invented. A conservative opaque podium top clears the original rendered terrain by at least 0.200001m across every intersecting grid cell; retaining sides extend below coarse grade, so no terrain/water opening is needed. Synthetic 10m residency clips much of the podium and removes the remote colonnade/entablature while native ground remains intact. Full architectural completeness requires the surrounding tiles. Neighboring Forum ruins still use generic procedural shells and their historical appearance is not approved. No bespoke survey, archive or academic research was used."
  },
